\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{A positive product gives the finite Hodge decomposition}
\date{2026-11-15}

\p{For real matrices #{A} and #{B} with #{AB=0}, and for the
positive coordinate inner product,
\citet{Theorem 5.2, p. 16}{lim2020hodgelaplacians}
states
#{\ker(A^{\dagger}A+BB^{\dagger})=\ker A\cap\ker B^{\dagger}}
and the middle space splits as
#{{\im}A^{\dagger}\oplus\ker(A^{\dagger}A+BB^{\dagger})\oplus{\im}B}.
\citet{Theorem 5.3, p. 16}{lim2020hodgelaplacians}
gives the isomorphism of that kernel with the cohomology
#{\ker A/{\im}B}. Taking #{A} to be the coboundary
leaving the degree and #{B} the coboundary entering it, this
is the orthogonal sum of \ref{fdip-0090}. For the Whitney
inner product, the same three statements are
\citet{(4.5)-(4.7), p. 94}{dodziuk1976hodge}: the harmonics
are the joint kernel, the cochain space is the orthogonal sum
of the two images and the harmonics, and the harmonics realize
the cohomology of the cochain complex. The opening of that section credits Eckmann,
\citet{p. 93}{dodziuk1976hodge}.}

\p{The hypothesis is a positive-definite inner product. Over
#{\mathbb{F}_2}, the matrix of
\citet{p. 17}{lim2020hodgelaplacians},
##{A=B=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&1\end{pmatrix},}
has #{AB=0} and #{A^{T}A+BB^{T}=0}, since each product entry
is #{1+1=0}. The kernel of the sum is the whole plane, while
#{\ker A\cap\ker B^{T}} is the line of vectors #{(x,x)}. The
sum of #{{\im}A^{T}}, the kernel of the sum, and
#{{\im}B} is not direct. The quotient
#{\ker A/{\im}B} is zero and the intersection is
not, so the isomorphism fails on this matrix. These are the
clauses the matrix kills. The prime-period form of the same
obstruction is \ref{fdip-00OM}. \ref{fdip-00OP} is the
ordered-field case proved for positive weights on the torus.}

\p{On an oriented triangulation, with real or complex cochains,
the operator #{K_D} of
\citet{Section 3, pp. 3-5}{catteralllaihounmuthyockey2018anomaly}
has kernel the harmonic cochains, isomorphic to the cohomology,
and its square is block diagonal by degree,
\citet{(3.8), p. 5}{catteralllaihounmuthyockey2018anomaly}.
\ref{fdip-0090} already gives the decomposition for a finite
real cochain complex with a positive-definite product.}

\p{The same source writes #{K_D=\partial-\bar\partial} in
\citet{(3.4), p. 4}{catteralllaihounmuthyockey2018anomaly}.
By the coefficient formulas,
\citet{(3.2), p. 4}{catteralllaihounmuthyockey2018anomaly}
sends a cochain on #{(p-1)}-simplices to a cochain on
#{p}-simplices, so the source's #{\partial} acts as
#{\covwedge}, and
\citet{(3.3), p. 4}{catteralllaihounmuthyockey2018anomaly}
is its counting transpose #{\extdag}. For the counting
product,
#{K_D={\covwedge}-\extdag={\covwedge}+{\covdot}=\cov},
where #{\extdag=-\covdot} and
#{\cov=\covdot+\covwedge}. That operator is
anti-self-adjoint. The negative sum of the two incidence
products in
\citet{(3.8), p. 5}{catteralllaihounmuthyockey2018anomaly}
equals #{K_D^2=\covsq}. The operator
#{\extD=\covwedge+\extdag} is self-adjoint and
#{\extD^2=-\covsq}. The two kernels are the harmonic
cochains of \ref{fdip-0090}. Outside the quoted formula,
#{\partial} is the chain boundary only.}
